# Passwords

Passwords are optional. Turn them on when the app starts:

```gleam
let assert Ok(identity) = auth.with_passwords(identity)
```

Without that call the password operations answer `403`, and the password
pages do not exist. Email tokens keep working for everyone, including people
who have a password: the two are alternative ways in, not two factors.

For a walk through a whole app, see
[Username and password sign-in](/docs/guides/username-and-password).

## Registering

`POST /password/register` takes an address and a password:

```json
{"email": "ada@example.com", "password": "a long and unusual passphrase"}
```

It answers `202` and emails a token. The account and its password only exist
once that token is exchanged at `POST /session` or `POST /token`, so an
account cannot be made for an address its owner does not control. Until
then only the password's hash is kept.

Registering an address that already has an account never replaces its
password, and never attaches one to an account that signs in by email. The
owner gets an `AlreadyRegistered` email instead; see
[Sending the email](/docs/auth/signing-in).

Because this request cannot be answered by a token already in the inbox,
repeating it for one address is slowed down instead: a 60 second wait after
the first, doubling up to an hour.

## Signing in

- `POST /password/session` with `{"email":"…","password":"…"}` answers with
  the user and sets the session cookie. Like `/session`, it requires your
  `origin`.
- `POST /password/token` takes the same body and answers with a bearer
  token.

A wrong password, an unknown address, an account without a password and a
suspended account all get the same `401`, after the same amount of work, so
the answer does not reveal which addresses have accounts.

## Setting, changing and resetting

One operation covers all three:

```
POST /password
{"password": "a new long and unusual passphrase"}
```

It only works from a session that was created by exchanging an email token
*within the last ten minutes*. A session made with a password, or an older
one, gets `403`. So whether somebody has forgotten their password, wants a
new one, or signed up by email and now wants a password, the steps are the
same:

1. Ask for a sign-in token at `POST /login`.
2. Exchange it at `POST /session`.
3. Send the new password to `POST /password`.

Every other session of that user is ended. The starter `/auth/account` page
does step three, and also lists sessions and signs out.

There is no "current password" check here, because the fresh email token is
stronger proof than the password it replaces.

## Changing with the current password

A user who knows their password can change it from any live session,
without an email round trip:

```
POST /password/change
{"current": "the old passphrase", "password": "a new long and unusual passphrase"}
```

This also works for apps without email tokens. A wrong current password is
a `400`, not a `401`, since the session itself is fine. Each guess counts
against the same limits as a sign-in, so a borrowed session cannot search
for the password any faster than the sign-in form allows. An account with
no password gets `403`, and uses `POST /password` instead.

Every other session and [remembered device](/docs/auth/mfa) is ended, and
the address is sent a `PasswordChanged` notice, whose token is empty. Tell
the reader to reset their password from a fresh email sign-in if the
change was not theirs; see [Sending the email](/docs/auth/signing-in).

## What makes a password acceptable

- At least 15 characters, counted in Unicode code points, and at most 1024
  bytes. The minimum is `password_min_length` in the
  [policy](/docs/auth/sessions), and cannot go below 8.
- Not on the built-in list of common passwords, and not an obvious shape: a
  common word with decoration, one unit repeated, a keyboard run, or very
  few distinct characters.
- Passwords are never trimmed. They are normalized to Unicode NFC, so the
  same password typed on different keyboards matches.

A refusal is a `400` with a message you can show.

The built-in list is small. To check against a corpus of breached
passwords, load one at startup:

```gleam
import howdy/auth/password_check

let identity =
  auth.with_password_check(identity, password_check.blocklist(corpus))
```

`corpus` is a list of passwords. The checker keeps only their SHA-256
digests, and sends nothing over the network. For anything else, pass your
own `fn(String) -> service.Result(Nil)`; it receives the new password, and
an error refuses it. The check applies when a password is set, not when an
existing one is used to sign in.

## Guessing

Each pair of address and client gets five attempts. After that, each
failure must wait: 60 seconds at first, doubling up to an hour. A success
clears the history for that pair only, and resetting the password clears it
for the address. A second, higher limit of 100 attempts a minute per
address catches an attacker who keeps changing client. All four numbers are
in the [policy](/docs/auth/sessions), and a refusal is a `429`.

The limits follow the client the routes identify, so
[behind a proxy](/docs/auth/administration) tell them which header to read.
Failed attempts on real accounts are recorded in the audit trail as
`login.failed`.

## Storage

Passwords are hashed with PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 from OTP's `crypto`: a fresh
16 byte salt and 600,000 iterations, as OWASP recommends. Only the encoded
hash is stored. A successful sign-in quietly upgrades a hash made with
weaker settings.

PBKDF2 is not memory-hard, so if your database leaked, guessing passwords
on GPUs would cost an attacker less than against Argon2id. The 15 character
minimum and the common-password check make up much of that difference. To
use another algorithm, implement `PasswordHasher` from
`howdy/auth/password_hasher` and enable passwords with
`auth.with_password_hasher(identity, hasher)` instead of
`auth.with_passwords`. Existing credentials must be readable by the hasher
you pick.

Hashing protects the passwords. It does not encrypt email addresses, the
audit trail, the database or its backups.

## Without HTTP

- `auth.register_password_from(identity, email, password, client)`
- `auth.login_password_from(identity, email, password, client)` returns the
  same `auth.Session` as `auth.exchange`
- `auth.set_password(identity, principal, password)`
- `auth.change_password_from(identity, principal, current:, new:, client:)`
- `auth.login_password_step`, when [second factors](/docs/auth/mfa) are on

As elsewhere, `register_password` and `login_password` without `_from`
share one client bucket between all callers.
